const {H1,H2,H3,P,STORY,CASE,TIP,DIG,SEC,LN,PB,TBL}=require("./lib");
const c=[];

// ---------- EP 07 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 07 — The Forgotten Prisoners: Amnesty International and the Protective Agencies"));
c.push(H2("Covers: The UN Commission on Human Rights (1946-2006) and the Human Rights Council (2006-) — the UPR, the Special Procedures, the complaint procedure · The Centre for Human Rights and the Office of the High Commissioner (OHCHR) · The treaty bodies · The ILO and human rights · UNICEF and human rights · UNHCR, UNESCO, WHO, UN Women · Non-governmental organisations — Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch · The International Committee of the Red Cross"));
c.push(STORY("London, 28 May 1961. The Observer published an article by a barrister, Peter Benenson, headed 'The Forgotten Prisoners'. It began with two Portuguese students who had been sentenced to seven years for raising their glasses in a Lisbon café in a toast to freedom, and went on to name a Romanian philosopher, a Greek trade unionist, an Angolan doctor, an American clergyman and a Hungarian cardinal — prisoners held for their opinions, whom Benenson called 'prisoners of conscience'. He proposed an 'Appeal for Amnesty, 1961': ordinary people would write letters to governments asking for the release of prisoners who had neither used nor advocated violence. The article was reprinted across Europe; within a month a thousand letters had arrived; within a year the appeal had become a permanent organisation with groups 'adopting' individual prisoners — one from the West, one from the Communist bloc, one from the developing world, to keep the work impartial. Amnesty International's research department documented cases; its candle wrapped in barbed wire became the movement's symbol; its campaigns against torture (1972) and the death penalty (1977) produced the UN Declaration against Torture (1975) and the Convention (1984); it won the Nobel Peace Prize in 1977; and its annual report became the standard of what a State's human-rights record is. The story is the lesson of this episode: the machinery of protection is not only the committees at Geneva but the organisations that feed them facts and the public that reads the reports."));
c.push(H3("The Commission on Human Rights, 1946-2006"));
c.push(P("Created by ECOSOC in 1946 under Art 68 of the Charter with 18 (later 53) member States elected by ECOSOC, meeting annually in Geneva for six weeks. Its first phase (1946-66) was **standard-setting** — the UDHR, the Covenants, the Declarations on the child (1959) and racial discrimination (1963) — under a self-denying 1947 decision that it had 'no power to take any action in regard to any complaints concerning human rights' (the thousands of petitions received were merely listed). The second phase (1967-) was **protection**: **ECOSOC Resolution 1235 (1967)** authorised the Commission to examine information on 'gross violations' and to study situations revealing 'a consistent pattern of violations' (aimed at South Africa and Rhodesia, then Chile after 1973, then generally) — the public procedure of country resolutions and rapporteurs; **ECOSOC Resolution 1503 (1970)** created the confidential procedure by which the Sub-Commission and the Commission examined communications 'appearing to reveal a consistent pattern of gross and reliably attested violations' — the first UN mechanism for individual complaints, confidential, slow and rarely decisive but a lever. From 1980 the **Special Procedures** grew: the Working Group on Enforced or Involuntary Disappearances (1980 — Argentina), the Special Rapporteurs on summary executions (1982), torture (1985), religious intolerance (1986), mercenaries, the sale of children (1990), freedom of expression (1993), violence against women (1994), the independence of judges (1994), racism (1993), and country mandates (Afghanistan, Iran, Myanmar, Sudan...). The **Sub-Commission on the Promotion and Protection of Human Rights** (1947-2006; 26 independent experts) did the studies (the Martínez Cobo study on indigenous peoples, the Whitaker report on genocide, the Norms on transnational corporations). The Commission was discredited by the 2000s by the election of gross violators (Sudan, Libya as chair in 2003), by selectivity and bloc voting, and by the annual fight over Israel; the Secretary-General's 2005 report 'In Larger Freedom' proposed its replacement."));
c.push(H3("The Human Rights Council, 2006-"));
c.push(P("**General Assembly Resolution 60/251 (15 March 2006)** created the **Human Rights Council** as a subsidiary organ of the General Assembly (not of ECOSOC — raising its status), with 47 members elected by the Assembly's absolute majority for three years (no more than two consecutive terms), on regional distribution (Africa 13, Asia-Pacific 13, Latin America and Caribbean 8, Western Europe and others 7, Eastern Europe 6), with the members' human-rights records to be taken into account and the Assembly able to suspend a member by two-thirds for gross violations (Libya 2011, Russia 2022); it meets in at least three regular sessions a year (ten weeks) and special sessions (over 35 so far — Darfur, Gaza, Syria, Myanmar, Ukraine, Iran). Its mechanisms (the 2007 'institution-building' resolution 5/1): (1) the **Universal Periodic Review** — every UN member's record examined every four and a half years by the Council's Working Group on the basis of the State's report, a UN compilation and a stakeholders' summary, in an interactive dialogue producing recommendations that the State accepts or notes (India was reviewed in 2008, 2012, 2017 and 2022, receiving 339 recommendations in 2022 and accepting 221 — on the death penalty, the UAPA, the FCRA, marital rape, the NHRC, the Rohingya); (2) the **Special Procedures** — inherited and expanded to 46 thematic and 14 country mandates (2024) — independent experts who receive complaints, send communications (urgent appeals and allegation letters) to States, undertake country visits with the State's invitation (India has issued few standing invitations; the Rapporteur on water visited in 2017), and report annually; (3) the **complaint procedure** (the successor of 1503 — the Working Group on Communications and the Working Group on Situations; confidential; consistent patterns of gross violations); (4) the **Advisory Committee** (18 experts, the successor of the Sub-Commission, with a limited research role); (5) the **Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples**, the Forum on Minority Issues, the Social Forum, the Forum on Business and Human Rights; (6) **commissions of inquiry and fact-finding missions** — Syria (2011-), Gaza (2009 — the Goldstone report; 2014; 2021 permanent), Sri Lanka (2014), North Korea (2013 — the report that found crimes against humanity), Eritrea, Burundi, Myanmar (2017 — the Rohingya findings that led to the Independent Investigative Mechanism and the Gambia v. Myanmar case), Venezuela, Ukraine (2022), Iran (2022), Sudan (2023); (7) resolutions, and the annual reports of the High Commissioner. The Council's record: the UPR's universality is its great advance (every State, including the permanent members, is reviewed); the Special Procedures are the system's eyes; the criticisms — the continued election of violators (China, Saudi Arabia, Cuba, Russia until 2022), the Israel 'Item 7', the United States' withdrawals (2018-21, 2025) — are the Commission's old politics."));
c.push(H3("The Centre for Human Rights and the OHCHR"));
c.push(P("The UN Secretariat's **Division of Human Rights** (New York, 1946, under John Humphrey) became the **Centre for Human Rights** in Geneva (1982) — the secretariat of the Commission, the Sub-Commission and the treaty bodies, with advisory services and a small staff. The **Vienna Conference (1993)** recommended, and **General Assembly Resolution 48/141 (20 December 1993)** created, the post of **United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights**: an Under-Secretary-General appointed by the Secretary-General with the Assembly's approval for four years (renewable once), with 'principal responsibility for United Nations human rights activities' — to promote and protect the effective enjoyment of all rights; to provide advisory services and technical assistance; to coordinate education and information; to play an active role in removing obstacles and in preventing violations; to engage in dialogue with Governments; to enhance international cooperation; to coordinate the UN system's human-rights activities; and to rationalise the machinery. The Centre was merged into the High Commissioner's office in 1997 to form the **Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights (OHCHR)**, Geneva (Palais Wilson), with a New York office, about 1,800 staff, regional and country offices (including field presences in conflict areas) and a budget largely of voluntary contributions. The High Commissioners: José Ayala Lasso (1994-97), Mary Robinson (1997-2002), Sergio Vieira de Mello (2002-03, killed in Baghdad), Louise Arbour (2004-08), Navi Pillay (2008-14), Zeid Ra'ad Al Hussein (2014-18), Michelle Bachelet (2018-22), Volker Türk (2022-). The Office's functions today: secretariat to the Council, the UPR, the Special Procedures and the ten treaty bodies; thematic research and guidance (the Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights, 2011; the Istanbul Protocol on torture documentation); technical cooperation and national institutions; monitoring and public reporting (the annual report, country reports — the 2018 and 2019 Kashmir reports which India rejected, the 2022 Xinjiang assessment); emergency response and human-rights components of peace operations; and the High Commissioner's voice — the 'conscience' role, with the limit that the Office cannot compel."));
c.push(H3("The treaty bodies"));
c.push(TBL([
["Treaty","Committee (members)","Procedures","India"],
["ICERD 1965","Committee on the Elimination of Racial Discrimination (18)","Reports; Art 14 individual communications (optional); early warning; inter-State (used 2018: Qatar v. UAE, Palestine v. Israel)","Party 1968; no Art 14 declaration; last report 2007"],
["ICCPR 1966","Human Rights Committee (18)","Reports; OP1 communications; OP2 death penalty","Party 1979; no OPs; reviewed 2024"],
["ICESCR 1966","Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (18, ECOSOC-created 1985)","Reports; OP 2008 communications and inquiry","Party 1979; no OP; reviewed 2008"],
["CEDAW 1979","Committee on the Elimination of Discrimination against Women (23)","Reports; OP 1999 communications and inquiry","Party 1993 with declarations; no OP; reviewed 2014"],
["CAT 1984","Committee against Torture (10); Subcommittee on Prevention (OPCAT 2002)","Reports; Art 20 inquiry; Art 22 communications (optional); OPCAT visits","Signed 1997, not ratified; Prevention of Torture Bill lapsed"],
["CRC 1989","Committee on the Rights of the Child (18)","Reports; OP3 2011 communications","Party 1992; OPs on armed conflict and sale of children 2005; reviewed 2014"],
["ICRMW 1990","Committee on Migrant Workers (14)","Reports; communications (not yet in force)","Not a party"],
["CRPD 2006","Committee on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (18)","Reports; OP communications and inquiry","Party 2007; no OP; reviewed 2019"],
["CED 2006","Committee on Enforced Disappearances (10)","Reports; urgent actions; communications (optional)","Signed 2007, not ratified"]
],[1500,2200,3100,2200]));
c.push(P("Common features: independent experts elected by the parties; **periodic State reports** examined in dialogue with **concluding observations**; **General Comments/Recommendations** interpreting the treaty; **individual communications** where the State has accepted (about 3,000 views across the bodies); **inquiries** into grave or systematic violations; follow-up procedures; the 'simplified reporting procedure'; the treaty-body strengthening process (General Assembly Resolution 68/268, 2014) to address the backlog of reports and the non-reporting of a third of States; and the chairs' annual meeting. Their weakness is the absence of enforcement — 'views' and 'observations' bind in good faith only — and their strength the jurisprudence and the domestic use of their findings by courts and NGOs."));
c.push(H3("The ILO and human rights"));
c.push(P("The **International Labour Organization** (Part XIII of the Treaty of Versailles, 1919; the Philadelphia Declaration 1944 — 'labour is not a commodity'; a UN specialised agency since 1946; the Nobel Peace Prize 1969; 187 members; Geneva) is the oldest human-rights institution and the only **tripartite** one: its International Labour Conference (annual), Governing Body and Office include governments, employers and workers (2:1:1). It has adopted 191 **Conventions** (binding on ratifying States) and 208 **Recommendations**. The **1998 Declaration on Fundamental Principles and Rights at Work** made four (since 2022 five) principles binding on all members by membership alone, embodied in the **fundamental Conventions**: freedom of association and collective bargaining (No 87 of 1948 and 98 of 1949); the elimination of forced labour (No 29 of 1930 and 105 of 1957, with the 2014 Protocol); the abolition of child labour (No 138 of 1973 on minimum age and 182 of 1999 on the worst forms — the first universally ratified Convention); the elimination of discrimination in employment (No 100 of 1951 on equal remuneration and 111 of 1958); and (2022) a safe and healthy working environment (No 155 of 1981 and 187 of 2006). India has ratified 47 Conventions including six of the ten fundamental ones (29, 105, 100, 111, 138 and 182 — the last two in 2017) but **not 87 and 98** (on the ground of the restrictions on civil servants' unionisation) nor 155/187. **Supervision**: the **Committee of Experts on the Application of Conventions and Recommendations** (20 jurists, since 1926) examines the reports on ratified Conventions (Art 22 of the Constitution) and publishes observations; the Conference's tripartite **Committee on the Application of Standards** discusses the worst cases; **representations** by employers' or workers' organisations (Art 24) and **complaints** by a State, a delegate or the Governing Body (Art 26) lead to a Commission of Inquiry (used a dozen times — Poland 1982, Myanmar 1997 on forced labour, leading to the Art 33 measures of 2000, the only time; Belarus 2003; Venezuela 2018); the **Committee on Freedom of Association** (1951) hears complaints on Conventions 87 and 98 even against non-ratifying States (over 3,400 cases); and the ILO's technical cooperation (IPEC on child labour; the Better Work programme). The ILO's human-rights standards on forced labour, child labour, discrimination, migrant workers (Nos 97 and 143), indigenous peoples (No 107 of 1957, which India ratified in 1958, and No 169 of 1989, which it has not), domestic workers (No 189 of 2011), violence and harassment at work (No 190 of 2019) and social security (No 102) are the substance of the second-generation rights, and the Indian Supreme Court has used them (Bandhua Mukti Morcha, 1984, on forced labour; M.C. Mehta v. State of Tamil Nadu, 1996, on child labour; Vishaka; Apparel Export Promotion Council)."));
c.push(H3("UNICEF and human rights"));
c.push(P("The **United Nations International Children's Emergency Fund** was created by the General Assembly on 11 December 1946 to feed and clothe the children of post-war Europe; made permanent in 1953 (keeping the acronym while becoming the United Nations Children's Fund); Nobel Peace Prize 1965; headquartered in New York with offices in over 190 countries; funded entirely by voluntary contributions (governments and the National Committees' private fundraising — the greeting cards). Its work moved from relief to development (the 1960s 'child survival' campaigns — oral rehydration, immunisation, breastfeeding, growth monitoring; the 1980s 'child survival and development revolution' under James Grant), and after the **Convention on the Rights of the Child (1989)** — which it helped draft and champions — to a **rights-based approach**: UNICEF's mission statement (1996) is 'to advocate for the protection of children's rights', and Art 45 of the Convention gives it a formal role in the Committee on the Rights of the Child's work (it may be represented, provide expert advice and receive the Committee's requests). Its human-rights functions: supporting States' reports and the Committee's reviews; the **World Summit for Children (1990)** and its goals, the 2002 Special Session ('A World Fit for Children'); birth registration (Art 7), education (Art 28 — the Education for All movement), protection from violence, exploitation and abuse (Arts 19, 32-36 — child labour, trafficking, child marriage, FGM), children in armed conflict (the Machel report 1996; the Paris Principles 2007 on child soldiers; the monitoring and reporting mechanism under Security Council Resolution 1612), juvenile justice (Art 40), the rights of the girl-child and children with disabilities, and emergency response. In India (since 1949) UNICEF works with the Government on immunisation (the polio eradication of 2014), nutrition (ICDS), water and sanitation (Swachh Bharat), education (the Right to Education Act 2009), child protection (the Integrated Child Protection Scheme, the JJ Act 2015, POCSO), and the State of the World's Children reports are its advocacy voice. UNICEF is not a monitoring or complaint body; its human-rights role is promotional and programmatic, in cooperation with the Committee and the Special Representatives on children and armed conflict and on violence against children."));
c.push(H3("The other UN bodies"));
c.push(P("**UNHCR** (1950; the Statute; the 1951 Convention's supervisory role under Art 35; Nobel 1954 and 1981; over 100 million forcibly displaced people of concern) — Episode 09. **UNESCO** (1945) — the right to education (the Convention against Discrimination in Education, 1960), the freedom of the press (the Windhoek Declaration 1991, World Press Freedom Day, the Safety of Journalists indicators), the 1974 Recommendation on education for peace and human rights, the Committee on Conventions and Recommendations' confidential communications procedure (the '104 EX/3.3' procedure since 1978) for violations of rights within UNESCO's fields, and cultural rights. **WHO** (1948 — its Constitution's preamble: 'the enjoyment of the highest attainable standard of health is one of the fundamental rights of every human being'; the Alma-Ata Declaration 1978; the Framework Convention on Tobacco Control 2003; the International Health Regulations; the Covid-19 pandemic's human-rights dimensions). **FAO** (the right to food — the 2004 Voluntary Guidelines). **UNDP** (the Human Development Reports since 1990 — Mahbub ul Haq and Sen; the 2000 report on human rights and development). **UN Women** (2010, merging UNIFEM, DAW, OSAGI and INSTRAW; the Commission on the Status of Women's secretariat). **UNFPA** (reproductive rights — Cairo 1994). **The Office of the Special Representatives** on children and armed conflict, sexual violence in conflict, and violence against children; the **Special Adviser on the Prevention of Genocide** (2004). **The UN Democracy Fund**, the **Voluntary Fund for Victims of Torture**, and the **Peacebuilding Commission**."));
c.push(H3("Amnesty International, Human Rights Watch and the NGOs"));
c.push(P("**Amnesty International** (London, 1961; Peter Benenson; the International Secretariat with about 3 million members and supporters in 150 countries; Nobel Peace Prize 1977; the UN Human Rights Prize 1978): its original mandate — the release of **prisoners of conscience** (persons imprisoned for their beliefs, colour, sex, ethnic origin, language or religion who have not used or advocated violence), fair and prompt trials for political prisoners, the abolition of torture and the death penalty, an end to extrajudicial executions and disappearances — expanded in 2001 to the full range of rights including economic and social rights, and to abuses by armed groups and companies; its methods — research (the fact-finding missions and the annual report), letter-writing and 'urgent actions', campaigns (Stop Torture, the Death Penalty, Arms Trade Treaty 2013, Control Arms, the 2005 'Stop Violence Against Women'), lobbying at the UN (ECOSOC consultative status; the drafting of the Torture Convention, the Rome Statute, the Optional Protocol to CAT), and public education; its impartiality rules (no work on one's own country's prisoners in the early decades; balanced adoption). In India Amnesty's country office was closed in 2020 after the Enforcement Directorate froze its accounts under the FCRA and FEMA — the Government alleging foreign-funding violations, Amnesty alleging reprisal for its Kashmir and Delhi-riots reports. **Human Rights Watch** (New York, 1978 — Helsinki Watch, monitoring the Soviet bloc's compliance with the Helsinki Final Act; Americas Watch 1981; merged 1988): investigative reports, advocacy with governments and the UN, the World Report, the campaign to ban landmines (Nobel 1997 with the ICBL), the Rome Statute coalition; it does not take members. Others: the **International Commission of Jurists** (1952; the rule of law; the Yogyakarta Principles), the **International Federation for Human Rights (FIDH, 1922)**, **Article 19**, the **Committee to Protect Journalists**, **Reporters Without Borders**, **Anti-Slavery International (1839 — the oldest)**, **Minority Rights Group**, **Survival International**, the **Open Society Justice Initiative**, and in India the **People's Union for Civil Liberties** (1976, Jayaprakash Narayan), the **People's Union for Democratic Rights** (1977), the **Commonwealth Human Rights Initiative** (1987, Delhi), the **Human Rights Law Network**, **SAHRDC**, and the many which litigate PILs. **NGOs' legal role**: consultative status with ECOSOC (Art 71 of the Charter; over 6,000); participation in the Council's sessions and the UPR (the stakeholders' submissions); the submission of 'shadow reports' to the treaty bodies; the 1503/complaint procedure and the Special Procedures' communications, for which they are the principal source; standing before the African Commission and the Inter-American Commission; amicus briefs; and the **1998 Declaration on Human Rights Defenders**, with its Special Rapporteur — the instrument that answers the FCRA-type restrictions."));
c.push(H3("The International Committee of the Red Cross"));
c.push(P("The **ICRC** (Geneva, 1863 — Henry Dunant's Committee of Five after Solferino; a private Swiss association governed by a Committee of up to 25 Swiss citizens; about 20,000 staff in 100 countries; Nobel Peace Prize 1917, 1944 and 1963 — the only triple laureate; observer status at the General Assembly since 1990) is the guardian of international humanitarian law and the founding component of the **International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement** (the ICRC; the 191 National Societies — the Indian Red Cross Society, 1920; and the International Federation, 1919 — with the Seven Fundamental Principles of 1965: humanity, impartiality, neutrality, independence, voluntary service, unity, universality; the emblems — the red cross, the red crescent and, since 2005, the red crystal — protected by Protocol III and by national law, in India the Geneva Conventions Act 1960 Sec 12-14). Its **mandate** comes from the Geneva Conventions themselves: to visit prisoners of war (GC III Art 126) and civilian internees (GC IV Art 143) without witnesses; to act as a substitute for the Protecting Power (GC I-IV common Art 10/10/10/11); to offer its services in non-international armed conflict (common Art 3 — the 'right of humanitarian initiative'); to run the **Central Tracing Agency** for missing persons and family messages (GC III Art 123, GC IV Art 140); to organise relief for civilians (GC IV Arts 59-63; AP I Art 70); and, under the Statutes of the Movement (1986), to work for the understanding and dissemination of IHL and to prepare its development — the ICRC drafted the 1949 Conventions and the 1977 Protocols, published the Commentaries (the updated Commentaries of 2016-20) and the **Customary IHL Study (2005)** of 161 rules. Its method is **confidentiality**: it reports violations to the party concerned, not publicly, so that it retains access (public denunciation only exceptionally, as with the Abu Ghraib leak in 2004 and the Gaza statements of 2023-24); its neutrality (its silence on the Holocaust, acknowledged in 1997, is the historic criticism). In India the ICRC has a regional delegation in New Delhi (1982), visited detainees in Jammu and Kashmir from 1995 to 2017 under an agreement with the Government, worked on the 1971 POWs' repatriation and the Kargil dead, and cooperates with the Indian Red Cross on tracing and disaster relief; India–Pakistan prisoner and fishermen exchanges go through it and the consular agreement of 2008. The ICRC is not a human-rights body — it applies IHL, not the Covenants — but the two bodies of law converge in occupation, detention and non-international conflict (Episode 10), and the ICRC's monitoring of detention is the most effective in the world because it is the least public."));
c.push(CASE("Human Rights Council Resolution 60/251 (GA, 2006) and Resolution 5/1 (2007)","The Council's constitution and the UPR, Special Procedures, complaint procedure and Advisory Committee; membership criteria and suspension."));
c.push(CASE("ILO Commission of Inquiry on Myanmar (1998) and the Art 33 resolution (2000)","Systematic forced labour in violation of Convention 29; the only invocation of Art 33 measures; the model of ILO enforcement."));
c.push(CASE("Bandhua Mukti Morcha v. Union of India (1984) 3 SCC 161","Bonded labour violates Art 21 and Art 23 and the ILO's forced-labour Conventions; the Court's socio-legal commissions; the State's duty to identify, release and rehabilitate."));
c.push(CASE("M.C. Mehta v. State of Tamil Nadu (1996) 6 SCC 756","Child labour in the Sivakasi match factories; the Court's scheme (compensation fund, education, employment for an adult of the family) drawing on Art 24, Art 45 and the CRC and ILO standards."));
c.push(CASE("Amnesty International India v. Enforcement Directorate (Karnataka HC 2022, and the 2024 adjudication)","The FCRA/FEMA proceedings that closed Amnesty's Indian operations — the domestic illustration of the Human Rights Defenders Declaration's concerns; the courts partly lifted the freezing orders."));
c.push(DIG("The Council's sessions are on UN Web TV; the UPR database and the Special Procedures' communications database are public; the OHCHR's 2023 'Universal Human Rights Index' links every recommendation to a State; Amnesty's Digital Verification Corps and HRW's Digital Investigations Lab use satellite imagery and open-source video (the Bucha and Sudan investigations); and the ICRC's 2023 rules of engagement for 'civilian hackers' and its position on cyber-operations extend the Geneva rules to the digital battlefield."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Human Rights Council', 'Universal Periodic Review', 'Special Procedures', '1503 procedure', 'High Commissioner for Human Rights', 'Treaty bodies', 'ILO's tripartism', 'ILO core Conventions', 'UNICEF', 'Amnesty International', 'Prisoner of conscience', 'ICRC's mandate', 'Fundamental Principles of the Red Cross'. Part B: 'Describe the UN machinery for the promotion and protection of human rights — the Council, the OHCHR and the treaty bodies'; or 'Discuss the role of the ILO in the protection of human rights and its supervisory machinery'; or 'Examine the contribution of UNICEF, Amnesty International and the ICRC to the protection of human rights'. Part C: a group of Indian NGOs wants the UN to examine custodial deaths in a State — the Special Rapporteur on torture's communication, the UPR stakeholders' submission, the Human Rights Committee's review (no individual complaint, as India is not a Protocol party), the NHRC; a trade union complains that a law bars government employees from unionising — the ILO Committee on Freedom of Association may hear it though India has not ratified Convention 87; a State refuses the ICRC access to detainees in an internal conflict — common Art 3 gives the ICRC only a right to offer; the State may refuse, but the Human Rights Council and the CAT (if a party) may act; a prisoner of conscience's family wants international help — Amnesty's urgent action, the Working Group on Arbitrary Detention (which takes complaints against every State)."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 08 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 08 — Bhanwari Devi: Vishaka, CEDAW and the Rights of Women and Children"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Women — from the UDHR to CEDAW (1979): the Convention's articles, the Committee, the Optional Protocol, the General Recommendations; DEVAW (1993), Beijing (1995), Security Council 1325; India's ratification and declarations; Vishaka and the 2013 Act · Children — the Geneva Declaration (1924), the UN Declaration of the Rights of the Child (1959), the Convention on the Rights of the Child (1989): the four principles and the articles, the three Optional Protocols, the Committee; India's law — the JJ Act 2015, POCSO 2012, the RTE Act 2009, child labour and marriage"));
c.push(STORY("Bhateri village, Rajasthan, 22 September 1992. Bhanwari Devi was a 'saathin' — a grassroots worker in the State Government's Women's Development Programme, paid a small honorarium to work against child marriage, dowry and the practices the Government wished to end. When she tried to stop the marriage of a nine-month-old girl in a Gujjar family, the village turned on her; her family was boycotted; and on that evening, as she and her husband worked in their field, five men beat him and gang-raped her. The police delayed the medical examination for fifty-two hours; the doctor recorded only her age; the trial court in Jaipur acquitted the accused in 1995 on grounds that included the improbability of upper-caste men raping a lower-caste woman and of a man raping in front of his relatives. The women's movement took the case to the Supreme Court in a public-interest petition in the name of Vishaka, a Rajasthan women's group, asking not for the retrial — which was on appeal — but for the enforcement of the right of working women to a safe workplace. On 13 August 1997 the Court, through Chief Justice Verma, held that sexual harassment at the workplace violates the rights to equality, life and dignity and to practise a profession under Articles 14, 15, 19(1)(g) and 21; that in the absence of legislation the Court would lay down guidelines under Article 32 that would bind as law until Parliament acted; and that the content of those guidelines was taken from India's obligations under the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women — Articles 11 and 24 and the CEDAW Committee's General Recommendation 19 on violence — read into the Constitution through Article 51(c). It took Parliament sixteen years to pass the Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention, Prohibition and Redressal) Act 2013. Bhanwari Devi's rapists were never convicted; her case remains the first in which an international women's-rights treaty became Indian law by judicial decision."));
c.push(H3("Women — from the Charter to CEDAW"));
c.push(P("The Charter (the Preamble's 'equal rights of men and women'; Art 8 — eligibility for all posts), the UDHR (Arts 2 and 16), the **Commission on the Status of Women** (1946, ECOSOC), the early conventions — the **Convention on the Political Rights of Women (1952)**, the **Convention on the Nationality of Married Women (1957)**, the **Convention on Consent to Marriage, Minimum Age for Marriage and Registration of Marriages (1962)**, the ILO's Conventions 100 (equal pay, 1951) and 111 (discrimination, 1958), UNESCO's 1960 Convention on education — and the **Declaration on the Elimination of Discrimination against Women (1967)** led, after the **International Women's Year (1975)** and the Mexico City conference, to the **Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women** (General Assembly, 18 December 1979; in force 3 September 1981; 189 parties — the United States has signed but not ratified; Iran, Somalia, Sudan, Tonga and the Holy See are outside; the most reserved-against human-rights treaty, with the Islamic States' Sharia reservations and the Committee's and other parties' objections under Art 28(2) — 'incompatible with the object and purpose')."));
c.push(H3("CEDAW — the articles"));
c.push(P("**Art 1** — 'discrimination against women' means any distinction, exclusion or restriction made on the basis of sex which has the effect or purpose of impairing or nullifying the recognition, enjoyment or exercise by women, irrespective of their marital status, on a basis of equality of men and women, of human rights and fundamental freedoms in the political, economic, social, cultural, civil or any other field (direct and indirect discrimination; public and private). **Art 2** — the parties condemn discrimination and undertake to embody equality in their constitutions, adopt legislation with sanctions, establish legal protection through tribunals, refrain from discrimination, take measures to eliminate discrimination by any person, organisation or enterprise, modify or abolish discriminatory laws, regulations, customs and practices, and repeal discriminatory penal provisions — the 'core' the Committee treats as non-reservable. **Art 3** — full development and advancement of women. **Art 4** — **temporary special measures** to accelerate de facto equality are not discrimination (affirmative action — General Recommendation 25), nor are measures to protect maternity. **Art 5** — the modification of social and cultural patterns of conduct to eliminate prejudices and practices based on the idea of the inferiority or superiority of either sex or on stereotyped roles (5(a)); the recognition of maternity as a social function and the common responsibility of men and women in the upbringing of children (5(b)). **Art 6** — suppression of trafficking and the exploitation of prostitution. **Art 7** — political and public life (vote, eligibility, public office, NGOs). **Art 8** — representation at the international level. **Art 9** — nationality (equal rights to acquire, change, retain; the children's nationality). **Art 10** — education (equal access, the same curricula, the elimination of stereotypes, scholarships, adult literacy, the reduction of drop-out, sports, family-planning information). **Art 11** — employment (the right to work, the same opportunities, free choice of profession, promotion, security, equal remuneration and treatment for work of equal value, social security, health and safety; the prohibition of dismissal for pregnancy or marriage, maternity leave with pay, child-care facilities, special protection during pregnancy). **Art 12** — health care including family planning; free services in pregnancy and confinement. **Art 13** — economic and social life (family benefits, credit, recreation). **Art 14** — **rural women** (the only human-rights treaty article on them — participation in development planning, health, social security, training, cooperatives, credit, housing, sanitation, electricity, water, transport). **Art 15** — equality before the law, legal capacity, contracts and property, freedom of movement and domicile. **Art 16** — marriage and family relations (the same right to enter marriage, free choice of spouse and consent, the same rights and responsibilities during marriage and at dissolution, as parents, to decide the number and spacing of children, guardianship and adoption, personal rights including the choice of name, profession and occupation, ownership of property; the betrothal and marriage of a child shall have no legal effect, with a minimum age and compulsory registration). **Part V — Arts 17-22** — the **Committee** (23 experts); reports within a year and every four years (Art 18); the Committee's reports and General Recommendations (Art 21); the specialised agencies. **Part VI — Arts 23-30** — more favourable national provisions; national implementation; signature and ratification; reservations (Art 28 — not incompatible with object and purpose); inter-State disputes to arbitration and the ICJ (Art 29 — India's reservation). The **Optional Protocol (1999; in force 2000; 115 parties; India not a party)** — individual and group communications after exhaustion of domestic remedies (A.T. v. Hungary, 2005, domestic violence; Vertido v. Philippines, 2010, rape-trial stereotypes; the Alyne da Silva Pimentel v. Brazil, 2011, maternal death; the first Indian-related view is not possible without ratification), and an **inquiry procedure** for grave or systematic violations (Mexico's Ciudad Juárez, 2005; the Philippines; Canada's missing indigenous women, 2015; Kyrgyzstan's bride kidnapping). The **General Recommendations** (40 so far): **GR 19 (1992)** — gender-based violence is discrimination under Art 1, and the State's failure to act with due diligence against private violence makes it responsible (the Velásquez Rodríguez standard); **GR 35 (2017)** updates it — violence as a social, not individual, problem, the prohibition as customary law; GR 21 (marriage and family), GR 24 (health), GR 25 (temporary special measures), GR 28 (Art 2 obligations), GR 30 (conflict), GR 33 (access to justice), GR 34 (rural women), GR 36 (education), GR 38 (trafficking), GR 39 (indigenous women)."));
c.push(H3("The later instruments"));
c.push(P("The **Declaration on the Elimination of Violence against Women (General Assembly, 1993)** — the first UN definition of violence against women (Art 1: any act of gender-based violence that results in, or is likely to result in, physical, sexual or psychological harm or suffering to women, including threats, coercion or arbitrary deprivation of liberty, in public or private life), its forms in the family, the community and by the State (Art 2), and the State's due-diligence duty (Art 4(c)); the **Special Rapporteur on violence against women (1994)**; the **Vienna Declaration (1993)** — 'the human rights of women and of the girl-child are an inalienable, integral and indivisible part of universal human rights'; the world conferences — Mexico City (1975), Copenhagen (1980), Nairobi (1985 — the Forward-looking Strategies), **Beijing (1995 — the Fourth World Conference: the Beijing Declaration and Platform for Action's twelve critical areas of concern, from poverty to the girl-child; 'gender mainstreaming'; the five-yearly reviews, Beijing+30 in 2025)**; the **Cairo Programme of Action (1994)** on reproductive rights; **Security Council Resolution 1325 (2000)** on women, peace and security (participation, protection, prevention, relief and recovery; the later resolutions 1820 (2008) on sexual violence as a tactic of war, 1888, 1889, 1960, 2106, 2122, 2242, 2467 (2019)); the **Rome Statute's** listing of rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, forced pregnancy, enforced sterilisation and other sexual violence as war crimes and crimes against humanity (Arts 7(1)(g), 8(2)(b)(xxii)); the **Palermo Protocol (2000)** on trafficking; the **Istanbul Convention (2011)** of the Council of Europe on violence against women; the **Belém do Pará Convention (1994)** and the **Maputo Protocol (2003)**; the **ILO Convention 190 (2019)** on violence and harassment at work; the **Sustainable Development Goal 5**; and **UN Women (2010)**."));
c.push(H3("India, CEDAW and the courts"));
c.push(P("India signed CEDAW on 30 July 1980 and ratified it on 9 July 1993, with **declarations**: (1) on **Arts 5(a) and 16(1)** — 'the Government of the Republic of India declares that it shall abide by and ensure these provisions in conformity with its policy of non-interference in the personal affairs of any Community without its initiative and consent' — the personal-law caveat; (2) on **Art 16(2)** — 'though in principle it fully supports the principle of compulsory registration of marriages, it is not practical in a vast country like India with its variety of customs, religions and level of literacy'; and a **reservation** on **Art 29(1)** (ICJ jurisdiction). The Committee has repeatedly asked India to withdraw the declarations (concluding observations 2000, 2007, 2014) and pressed on personal laws, the Uniform Civil Code, the Gujarat and Manipur violence, marital rape (the exception in Sec 375 IPC/Sec 63 BNS), dowry deaths, sex-selective abortion, honour killings, the Armed Forces (Special Powers) Act, and the two-child norm; India's last report was reviewed in 2014 and the next is overdue. **The courts**: **Vishaka (1997)** — CEDAW Arts 11, 24 and GR 19 read into Arts 14, 15, 19(1)(g), 21 and 32 to lay down the sexual-harassment guidelines (definition, the employer's duty, complaints committees headed by a woman with a third-party member, disciplinary action, criminal complaints, awareness); **Apparel Export Promotion Council v. A.K. Chopra (1999)** — the dismissal of an officer for attempted molestation upheld; the 'international instruments cast an obligation on the Indian State to gender-sensitise its laws'; **Madhu Kishwar v. State of Bihar (1996)** — the Chotanagpur Tenancy Act's exclusion of tribal women from inheritance was not struck down but the majority gave them a right to hold the land during their lifetime, citing CEDAW and the Vienna Declaration; **C. Masilamani Mudaliar v. Idol of Sri Swaminathaswami (1996)** — women's property rights under Sec 14 of the Hindu Succession Act read with CEDAW and the UDHR; **Githa Hariharan v. RBI (1999)** — the mother as natural guardian, citing CEDAW and the Beijing Declaration; **Municipal Corporation of Delhi v. Female Workers (Muster Roll) (2000)** — maternity benefit for daily-wage women, citing Art 11 CEDAW and the UDHR; **Air India v. Nergesh Meerza (1981)** (before ratification, the pregnancy-termination rule struck down under Art 14); **Anuj Garg v. Hotel Association of India (2008)** — protective discrimination against women bartenders struck down as stereotyping (Art 5(a) logic); **Charu Khurana v. Union of India (2015)** — the film make-up artists' association's ban on women members struck down; **Shayara Bano v. Union of India (2017)** — triple talaq unconstitutional (the minority referred to CEDAW; the majority relied on arbitrariness); **Joseph Shine v. Union of India (2018)** — adultery (Sec 497 IPC) struck down as treating the wife as property (Art 16 CEDAW); **Indian Young Lawyers Association v. State of Kerala (Sabarimala, 2018)**; **Secretary, Ministry of Defence v. Babita Puniya (2020)** — permanent commission for women army officers, rejecting stereotypes (Art 5(a)); **X v. Principal Secretary, Health (2022)** — unmarried women's abortion rights and the recognition of marital rape for MTP purposes. The 2013 Act, the Protection of Women from Domestic Violence Act 2005 (which cites CEDAW in its Statement of Objects — the first Indian Act to do so), the Criminal Law (Amendment) Act 2013 after the Delhi gang-rape (the Verma Committee's report used CEDAW and the international standards), the Maternity Benefit (Amendment) Act 2017 (26 weeks), the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act 2006, the Dowry Prohibition Act 1961, the PCPNDT Act 1994, the Hindu Succession (Amendment) Act 2005 (daughters as coparceners — Vineeta Sharma, 2020), the Constitution (106th Amendment) Act 2023 (one-third reservation for women in Parliament and Assemblies, pending delimitation), and the 73rd and 74th Amendments' panchayat reservations are the legislative side."));
c.push(H3("Children — from Geneva 1924 to the Convention of 1989"));
c.push(P("**Eglantyne Jebb's Save the Children Fund** (1919) drafted the **Geneva Declaration of the Rights of the Child (1924)**, adopted by the League of Nations — five principles: the child must be given the means for normal development; must be fed, nursed, reclaimed, sheltered; must be the first to receive relief in distress; must be protected against exploitation; must be brought up in the consciousness of service. The UDHR's Art 25(2) ('motherhood and childhood are entitled to special care and assistance'). The **UN Declaration of the Rights of the Child (General Assembly, 20 November 1959)** — ten principles: (1) all rights without discrimination; (2) special protection and opportunities for development, 'the best interests of the child shall be the paramount consideration'; (3) a name and nationality; (4) social security, nutrition, housing, recreation and medical services, pre-natal and post-natal care; (5) special treatment for the handicapped; (6) love and understanding, the care of parents, no separation from the mother in tender years except in exceptional circumstances, society's duty to the family-less; (7) free and compulsory education at least in the elementary stages, play and recreation; (8) first to receive protection and relief; (9) protection against neglect, cruelty and exploitation, no employment before a minimum age; (10) protection from discrimination, upbringing in tolerance and peace. The **International Year of the Child (1979)** and Poland's proposal led to ten years of drafting and the **Convention on the Rights of the Child (General Assembly, 20 November 1989; in force 2 September 1990)** — the most ratified human-rights treaty (196 parties — every State except the United States)."));
c.push(H3("The Convention on the Rights of the Child"));
c.push(P("**Art 1** — a child is every human being below eighteen unless majority is attained earlier under the applicable law. **The four general principles** (the Committee's General Comment 5): **non-discrimination (Art 2)**; **the best interests of the child as a primary consideration in all actions** (Art 3 — GC 14); **the right to life, survival and development (Art 6)**; and **respect for the views of the child** — the right to be heard in all matters affecting the child, given due weight in accordance with age and maturity, including in judicial and administrative proceedings (Art 12 — GC 12). **The rights**, conventionally grouped as the '4 Ps' — provision, protection, participation, prevention — or as: **civil rights and freedoms** — name, nationality and registration at birth (Art 7), preservation of identity (Art 8), expression (13), thought, conscience and religion (14), association (15), privacy (16), access to information and the media's role (17), no torture or capital punishment or life without parole for under-eighteens (37(a)); **family environment and alternative care** — no separation from parents except in the child's best interests (9), family reunification (10), no illicit transfer abroad (11), parents' common responsibilities and State support (18), protection from all forms of violence, abuse and neglect (19), alternative care for the child deprived of a family (20), adoption with the best interests as the paramount consideration and inter-country adoption as a last resort (21 — India's Hague Convention obligations, the CARA system), refugee children (22), periodic review of placement (25), recovery of maintenance (27(4)); **basic health and welfare** — the disabled child (23), the highest attainable standard of health (24 — with the abolition of harmful traditional practices, 24(3)), social security (26), an adequate standard of living (27); **education, leisure and culture** — education, free and compulsory primary, secondary available, discipline consistent with dignity (28), the aims of education (29), minority and indigenous children's culture (30), rest, play and culture (31); **special protection measures** — refugee children (22), children in armed conflict (38 — no recruitment under fifteen; raised to eighteen by the 2000 Protocol), economic exploitation and child labour (32), drugs (33), sexual exploitation and abuse (34), abduction, sale and trafficking (35), other exploitation (36), torture, deprivation of liberty as a last resort and for the shortest time, separation from adults, contact with family, legal assistance (37), recovery and reintegration of victims (39), **juvenile justice** (40 — the presumption of innocence, notice, a fair hearing, no self-incrimination, appeal, interpreter, privacy; a minimum age of criminal responsibility; diversion; a variety of dispositions as alternatives to institutional care). **Art 4** — implementation to the maximum extent of available resources for economic, social and cultural rights; **Art 5** — the evolving capacities of the child and parents' guidance; **Art 41** — more favourable national law. **Arts 42-45** — the **Committee on the Rights of the Child** (18 experts) — reports within two years and every five years, General Comments (26 — on HIV, adolescent health, early childhood, the best interests, the right to be heard, violence, business, the digital environment (GC 25, 2021), the environment and climate (GC 26, 2023)), and UNICEF's and the agencies' participation. **The Optional Protocols**: (1) **on the involvement of children in armed conflict (2000; in force 2002)** — no compulsory recruitment under eighteen, no direct participation in hostilities under eighteen, voluntary recruitment by States above fifteen with safeguards, armed groups not to recruit under eighteen; (2) **on the sale of children, child prostitution and child pornography (2000; in force 2002)** — criminalisation, jurisdiction, extradition, victim protection; (3) **on a communications procedure (2011; in force 2014; 52 parties; India not a party)** — individual communications by or on behalf of children, inter-State, and inquiries (Sacchi v. Argentina et al., 2021 — the climate communication by Greta Thunberg and fifteen children, inadmissible for non-exhaustion but recognising extra-territorial jurisdiction for climate harm)."));
c.push(H3("India and the rights of the child"));
c.push(P("India acceded to the Convention on 11 December 1992 with a **declaration on Art 32** (child labour): 'the Government of India undertakes to take measures to progressively implement the provisions of Art 32, particularly paragraph 2(a), in accordance with its national legislation and relevant international instruments to which it is a State Party' — that is, a minimum age only progressively; ratified the first two Optional Protocols in 2005; is not a party to the third. **The constitutional provisions**: Art 15(3) (special provisions for children), Art 21A (free and compulsory education for six to fourteen — the 86th Amendment 2002; the Right of Children to Free and Compulsory Education Act 2009 — Society for Unaided Private Schools v. Union of India, 2012, upheld the 25 per cent quota; Pramati, 2014, exempted minority schools), Art 23 (trafficking and forced labour), Art 24 (no child under fourteen in factories, mines or hazardous employment), Art 39(e)-(f) (health and strength of children not abused; opportunities for healthy development, protection against exploitation and moral and material abandonment), Art 45 (early childhood care under six, after 2002), Art 47, Art 51A(k) (the parents' duty to provide education). **The statutes**: the **Juvenile Justice (Care and Protection of Children) Act 2015** (replacing 1986 and 2000 — the Convention in its Preamble; children in conflict with law and children in need of care and protection; the Juvenile Justice Boards and Child Welfare Committees; the 2015 provision for trial of sixteen to eighteen-year-olds as adults for heinous offences after a preliminary assessment (Sec 15, after the Delhi gang-rape — criticised by the CRC Committee as contrary to Art 40); adoption under Secs 56-73 and CARA; Sec 74 — no disclosure of identity; foster care; the 2021 amendment — District Magistrates' powers); the **Protection of Children from Sexual Offences Act 2012** (gender-neutral offences of penetrative and aggravated sexual assault, sexual harassment, pornography; child-friendly procedures — Secs 24-33; Special Courts; the presumption of Sec 29; the 2019 amendment's death penalty for aggravated penetrative assault; Sec 23 on the media); the **Child Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act 1986**, amended in 2016 as the Child and Adolescent Labour (Prohibition and Regulation) Act — a complete prohibition under fourteen (with the contested exceptions for family enterprises and child artists) and of adolescents (fourteen to eighteen) in hazardous occupations, after India's ratification of ILO Conventions 138 and 182 in 2017; the **Prohibition of Child Marriage Act 2006** (voidable marriages, punishment of adults, the 2021 Bill to raise women's age to twenty-one lapsed; Independent Thought v. Union of India, 2017 — sexual intercourse with a wife under eighteen is rape, reading down the IPC exception); the **Commissions for Protection of Child Rights Act 2005** (the NCPCR and the State Commissions — India's Art 4 institutions); the **Bonded Labour System (Abolition) Act 1976**; the **Immoral Traffic (Prevention) Act 1956**; the **PCPNDT Act 1994** (the girl-child); the **Infant Milk Substitutes Act 1992**; the Integrated Child Development Services (1975) and the mid-day meal (PUCL, 2001 — the Court's order making cooked meals compulsory), the National Policy for Children (2013), the Integrated Child Protection Scheme (2009, now Mission Vatsalya), the National Commission's ban on corporal punishment (RTE Act Sec 17). **The cases**: M.C. Mehta v. State of Tamil Nadu (1996), Bandhua Mukti Morcha (1984, 1997 — the carpet-weaving children of Mirzapur), Sheela Barse v. Union of India (1986 — children in jails), Lakshmi Kant Pandey v. Union of India (1984 — inter-country adoption guidelines, citing the 1959 Declaration and the draft Convention), Gaurav Jain v. Union of India (1997 — prostitutes' children), Vishal Jeet v. Union of India (1990 — child prostitution), Sampurna Behura v. Union of India (2018 — the implementation of the JJ Act), Exploitation of Children in Orphanages in Tamil Nadu (2017), Salil Bali v. Union of India (2013 — the JJ Act's age of eighteen upheld after the Delhi case), Alakh Alok Srivastava v. Union of India (2018 — POCSO trial timelines), Pratap Singh v. State of Jharkhand (2005 — the date of offence for juvenility), and the 2018-24 directions on child-care institutions. The CRC Committee's 2014 concluding observations on India asked for the withdrawal of the Art 32 declaration, the ratification of the third Protocol, a ban on corporal punishment, an end to the 'adult trial' of sixteen-year-olds, and the reform of the AFSPA's application to children."));
c.push(CASE("Vishaka v. State of Rajasthan (1997) 6 SCC 241","Sexual harassment at the workplace violates Arts 14, 15, 19(1)(g) and 21; CEDAW Arts 11 and 24 and GR 19 read into the Constitution; binding guidelines under Art 32 until legislation — the 2013 Act."));
c.push(CASE("Apparel Export Promotion Council v. A.K. Chopra (1999) 1 SCC 759","Attempted molestation is sexual harassment; the courts must not be swayed by 'no physical contact'; international instruments oblige gender-sensitive interpretation."));
c.push(CASE("Githa Hariharan v. Reserve Bank of India (1999) 2 SCC 228","'After' in Sec 6 of the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act read as 'in the absence of' so that the mother is a natural guardian; CEDAW and the Beijing Declaration cited; the Convention on the Rights of the Child's best-interests principle applied."));
c.push(CASE("Independent Thought v. Union of India (2017) 10 SCC 800","Exception 2 to Sec 375 IPC read down: sexual intercourse with a wife aged fifteen to eighteen is rape; the CRC, the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act and POCSO harmonised; the child's best interests."));
c.push(CASE("Lakshmi Kant Pandey v. Union of India (1984) 2 SCC 244","Guidelines for inter-country adoption to prevent trafficking, drawing on the 1959 Declaration and the draft Convention; the child's welfare paramount; CARA created in consequence."));
c.push(CASE("A.T. v. Hungary, CEDAW Committee 2/2003 (2005)","The State's failure to protect a woman from her violent ex-partner (no protection orders, no shelters) violated Arts 2, 5 and 16; the first communication decided under the Optional Protocol."));
c.push(DIG("The CRC Committee's General Comment 25 (2021) on children's rights in the digital environment — privacy, protection from online abuse, access, the platforms' duties — and India's IT Rules 2021 and the DPDP Act's Sec 9 (verifiable parental consent, no tracking of children) are the current interface; POCSO's Sec 14-15 on child sexual abuse material and the 2024 Supreme Court ruling in Just Rights for Children Alliance v. S. Harish (storing and viewing CSAM is an offence) apply it; and the CEDAW Committee's GR 35 addresses online gender-based violence, which the IT Rules' 24-hour takedown of intimate images implements."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'Definition of discrimination under CEDAW Art 1', 'Temporary special measures (Art 4)', 'Art 5(a) CEDAW', 'CEDAW Committee', 'General Recommendation 19', 'India's declarations to CEDAW', 'Vishaka case', 'DEVAW 1993', 'Beijing Platform', 'Resolution 1325', '1959 Declaration', 'Four principles of the CRC', 'Best interests of the child', 'Art 12 CRC', 'Optional Protocols to the CRC', 'India's declaration on Art 32'. Part B: 'Discuss the salient features of CEDAW and its implementation machinery, and examine India's obligations and reservations'; or 'How has the Indian Supreme Court used CEDAW? Discuss with Vishaka and later cases'; or 'Trace the development of the international law on the rights of the child from the Geneva Declaration to the Convention of 1989, and examine the Convention's principles and India's implementation'. Part C: an employer has no internal complaints committee — Vishaka and the 2013 Act (Sec 4, penalty Sec 26); a personal law denies a woman an equal share — India's Art 16 declaration, but Arts 14-15 apply (Vineeta Sharma; Shayara Bano); a State employs children of twelve in a family beedi enterprise — Art 32 CRC, the 2016 Act's family-enterprise exception is criticised, but beedi is hazardous — prohibited; a sixteen-year-old is tried as an adult — Sec 15 JJ Act, the CRC Committee's objection, the preliminary assessment's safeguards; a girl of sixteen is married and her husband claims the marital exception — Independent Thought, rape."));
c.push(PB());

// ---------- EP 09 ----------
c.push(H1("Episode 09 — The Mountain of the Dongria: Niyamgiri, Indigenous Peoples, Refugees and the Other Vulnerable Groups"));
c.push(H2("Covers: Indigenous peoples — ILO Conventions 107 and 169, the UN Declaration (2007), the Permanent Forum, the Expert Mechanism, free prior and informed consent; India's position — Scheduled Tribes, the Fifth and Sixth Schedules, PESA, the Forest Rights Act, Samatha and Niyamgiri · Refugees — the 1951 Convention and 1967 Protocol, the definition, non-refoulement, exclusion and cessation, UNHCR; India's non-accession and practice — the Chakma, Sri Lankan, Tibetan and Rohingya cases, the CAA · Internally displaced persons, stateless persons, migrant workers, minorities, persons with disabilities, older persons"));
c.push(STORY("Niyamgiri hills, Odisha, July 2013. The Dongria Kondh, a 'particularly vulnerable tribal group' of about eight thousand, live in the forests of the Niyamgiri range in Rayagada and Kalahandi districts and worship the summit, Niyam Raja, as their god and lawgiver. Under the hills lies bauxite, and in 2004 the Odisha Mining Corporation and Vedanta Resources agreed to mine it for Vedanta's alumina refinery at Lanjigarh. The environmental clearance was challenged before the Supreme Court's Central Empowered Committee, granted in 2008 on conditions, and withdrawn by the Environment Ministry in 2010 on the Saxena Committee's report that the mining would destroy the Dongria's habitat and violate the Forest Rights Act. The Corporation went to the Supreme Court. In April 2013 the Court, through Justice Radhakrishnan, held that the Forest Rights Act 2006 recognises the tribals' religious and cultural rights over their traditional forests, that the gram sabhas of the affected villages must decide, under Section 4(d) and 6 of the Act and Section 4 of PESA, whether the mining would infringe those rights — including the right to worship Niyam Raja — and that the Ministry must take the gram sabhas' decision into account. The Court cited the ILO's Convention 169, the UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples and the 'free, prior and informed consent' standard of the Inter-American Court's Saramaka judgment, while noting India's position that it has no 'indigenous peoples' as such. In July and August 2013 twelve gram sabhas met — each under a judicial officer, with the villagers speaking in Kui — and every one voted against the mine. The Ministry rejected the clearance in January 2014. Niyamgiri is the Indian case in which the international law of indigenous peoples was applied through the domestic statute, and its outcome — a consultation that was allowed to mean 'no' — is what the Declaration's Article 32 asks for."));
c.push(H3("Indigenous peoples — the international law"));
c.push(P("The League's mandates and the ILO's early conventions on 'native labour' were the first attention; the **ILO Convention 107 (1957) concerning the Protection and Integration of Indigenous and Other Tribal and Semi-Tribal Populations** — the first binding instrument, with an assimilationist premise ('integration') but with protection of land rights (Art 11 — the right of ownership, collective or individual, over traditionally occupied land; Art 12 — no removal without free consent except by law for national security, development or health, with compensating land) — ratified by 27 States including India (1958), Pakistan and Bangladesh, and closed to ratification after the **ILO Convention 169 (1989) concerning Indigenous and Tribal Peoples**, which replaced integration with respect for the peoples' own institutions and ways of life: self-identification (Art 1(2)); the right to decide their own priorities for development (Art 7); consultation through their representative institutions 'with the objective of achieving agreement or consent' whenever legislative or administrative measures may affect them (Art 6); customary law (Arts 8-9); land rights — recognition of ownership and possession of traditionally occupied lands and safeguarding of their right to use lands to which they have traditionally had access (Art 14), natural resources (Art 15 — consultation before exploration or exploitation of sub-surface resources the State owns; participation in benefits; compensation), no relocation except with free and informed consent or by procedures with a right of return (Art 16); employment, vocational training, social security, health, education in their own languages, cross-border contacts — ratified by 24 States, mostly Latin American (Norway, Denmark, the Netherlands, Spain, Nepal and the Central African Republic elsewhere); India has not ratified it. The **Sub-Commission's Working Group on Indigenous Populations (1982-2006)** and the **Martínez Cobo study** (the working definition: indigenous communities are those having a historical continuity with pre-invasion and pre-colonial societies, considering themselves distinct from the dominant sectors, and determined to preserve their ancestral territories and identity) produced the draft Declaration (1993); the two International Decades (1995-2004, 2005-14); the **Permanent Forum on Indigenous Issues (ECOSOC, 2000)**; the **Special Rapporteur (2001)**; and the **UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples (General Assembly, 13 September 2007; 144 for, 4 against — Australia, Canada, New Zealand and the United States, all of which later endorsed it — 11 abstentions; India voted in favour)** — 46 articles: self-determination (Art 3, within the State — Art 46(1)'s territorial-integrity clause), autonomy in internal and local affairs (4), their own institutions (5), no forced assimilation or destruction of culture (8), no forced removal without free, prior and informed consent (10), cultural and religious rights and repatriation of remains (11-12), languages and education (13-14), media (16), labour (17), participation in decision-making (18), consultation to obtain free, prior and informed consent before adopting measures that may affect them (19), subsistence and development (20), improvement of economic and social conditions (21), women and children (22), health (24), spiritual relationship with lands (25), **the right to the lands, territories and resources they have traditionally owned, occupied or used, with legal recognition and protection (26)**, adjudication (27), redress and restitution (28), conservation of the environment (29), no military activities (30), intellectual property and traditional knowledge (31), **free, prior and informed consent before the approval of any project affecting their lands, particularly the development, utilisation or exploitation of mineral, water or other resources (32)**, identity and membership (33), juridical systems (34), treaties (37), the State's duty to implement (38), financial assistance (39), dispute resolution (40), the UN's role (41-42 — the **Expert Mechanism on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples**, 2007). The **case law**: Awas Tingni, Saramaka and Sarayaku (Inter-American — FPIC and communal property), Endorois and Ogiek (African), Lovelace and Kitok (Human Rights Committee, Art 27), Poma Poma v. Peru (2009 — water diversion without consultation violated Art 27), and the CERD's General Recommendation 23 (1997)."));
c.push(H3("India's position and law"));
c.push(P("India's official position (at the Working Group, the Declaration's vote and the Permanent Forum) is that all Indians are indigenous — the concept of 'indigenous peoples' as distinct from settlers does not apply, since India's population is the product of millennia of migration and mixing — and that its **Scheduled Tribes** (Art 342; 705 communities, 8.6 per cent of the population, 10.4 crore in 2011) are the beneficiaries of its constitutional protection; it ratified Convention 107, has not ratified 169, voted for the Declaration with an explanation, and is reviewed by the CERD (which in 2007 called the Scheduled Tribes indigenous). The **constitutional scheme**: Arts 15(4), 16(4) and 46 (educational and economic interests, protection from social injustice and exploitation), 19(5) (restrictions on movement and residence for the protection of Scheduled Tribes), 29 (culture), 244 and the **Fifth Schedule** (the administration of Scheduled Areas in ten States — the Governor's powers to modify laws and to regulate land transfer and money-lending, Tribes Advisory Councils) and the **Sixth Schedule** (the autonomous district and regional councils of Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram with legislative, judicial and financial powers over land, forests, customs and inheritance — the closest Indian analogue of self-government), Art 275(1) grants, Art 330 and 332 reservation, Art 338A the National Commission for Scheduled Tribes (2003), Art 371A (Nagaland) and 371G (Mizoram) — no Act of Parliament on Naga/Mizo religious or social practices, customary law, land and resources applies unless the Assembly so decides. The **statutes**: the State land-transfer regulations (the A.P. Scheduled Areas Land Transfer Regulation 1959 as amended by Regulation 1 of 1970 — no transfer of land in Scheduled Areas to non-tribals, upheld and extended to Government leases in **Samatha v. State of A.P. (1997)** — the Court held that mining leases to non-tribals in the Scheduled Areas of Visakhapatnam were void, that 'person' includes the Government, and cited the ILO Conventions and the Rio Declaration; the Telangana and A.P. Governments' subsequent attempts to amend the Regulation), the **Panchayats (Extension to the Scheduled Areas) Act 1996 (PESA)** — the gram sabha's powers over minor forest produce, land acquisition (mandatory consultation), minor minerals (recommendation), money-lending, intoxicants, and the preservation of traditions (Sec 4), the **Scheduled Tribes and Other Traditional Forest Dwellers (Recognition of Forest Rights) Act 2006** (the **Forest Rights Act** — individual and community rights over forest land occupied before 13 December 2005, the right to protect and manage community forest resources (Sec 3(1)(i)), the right to habitat for PVTGs (3(1)(e)), the gram sabha as the authority (Sec 6), and Sec 4(5) — no eviction until the recognition process is complete; the 2019 Wildlife First order for eviction of rejected claimants, stayed; the Court's 2024 status), the **Right to Fair Compensation and Transparency in Land Acquisition, Rehabilitation and Resettlement Act 2013** (Sec 41 — Scheduled Areas acquisition only as a last resort with the gram sabha's consent; Sec 42), the **SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act 1989**, the Mines and Minerals Act's 2015 District Mineral Foundations. **The cases**: Samatha (1997); **Orissa Mining Corporation v. Ministry of Environment (Niyamgiri, 2013)**; Kailas v. State of Maharashtra (2011 — Justice Katju's account of the tribals as India's original inhabitants); Wildlife First v. Ministry (2019, stayed); Narmada Bachao Andolan v. Union of India (2000 — the Sardar Sarovar rehabilitation of tribal oustees under the Tribunal award); the Kerala (Kalliyasseri) and the Chhattisgarh Salwa Judum case (Nandini Sundar v. State of Chhattisgarh, 2011 — the arming of tribal youth as SPOs violated Arts 14 and 21); the Meghalaya and Nagaland Sixth Schedule and Art 371A litigation; the Andaman's Jarawa protection (the Andaman and Nicobar Islands (Protection of Aboriginal Tribes) Regulation 1956). The **gap** between the international standard (FPIC, self-identification, resource rights) and Indian practice (consultation, State ownership of minerals, the Forest Conservation (Amendment) Act 2023's exemptions) is the examiner's discussion point."));
c.push(H3("Refugees — the international law"));
c.push(P("The League's High Commissioner for Russian refugees (Nansen, 1921 — the Nansen passport), the 1933 and 1938 Conventions, the IRO (1946-52) and the **UNHCR** (General Assembly Resolution 428(V), 14 December 1950; the Statute — a non-political, humanitarian office providing international protection and seeking permanent solutions (voluntary repatriation, local integration, resettlement); Geneva; about 20,000 staff; the 'persons of concern' now including IDPs, the stateless and returnees; the Executive Committee's Conclusions; India joined the Executive Committee in 1995 and hosts UNHCR's office in Delhi, which registers and assists refugees the Government does not recognise). The **Convention relating to the Status of Refugees (28 July 1951; in force 1954)** was limited to events before 1 January 1951 and (optionally) to Europe; the **1967 Protocol** removed the limits (149 parties to one or both; India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Sri Lanka, Nepal, Myanmar, Thailand, Malaysia, Indonesia are not parties — South Asia's absence is the striking gap). **Art 1A(2) — the definition**: a refugee is a person who, owing to a **well-founded fear of being persecuted** for reasons of **race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group or political opinion**, is outside the country of his nationality and is unable or, owing to such fear, unwilling to avail himself of its protection (or, being stateless, is outside his former habitual residence and unable or unwilling to return) — the five grounds; the subjective fear and the objective basis; persecution (serious harm plus a failure of State protection — by the State or by non-State actors the State cannot or will not control); the 'particular social group' (women, sexual minorities, families — the Acosta and Ward tests; UNHCR's Guidelines); economic migrants, victims of generalised violence and natural disasters and 'climate refugees' are outside unless persecution is shown (the regional definitions of the **OAU Convention (1969)** — persons fleeing external aggression, occupation, foreign domination or events seriously disturbing public order — and the **Cartagena Declaration (1984)** — generalised violence, massive violation of human rights — are wider; Teitiota v. New Zealand (HRC 2020) opened the Covenant's Art 6 to climate displacement). **Art 1C — cessation** (voluntary re-availment, re-acquisition, new nationality, re-establishment, the circumstances have ceased); **Art 1D** (Palestinians under UNRWA), **1E** (rights of nationals in the residence country); **Art 1F — exclusion**: serious reasons for considering that the person has committed a crime against peace, a war crime or a crime against humanity, a serious non-political crime outside the country of refuge, or acts contrary to the UN's purposes. **The rights** (Arts 3-34, on a sliding scale — the same as nationals for religion, courts, elementary education, public relief, labour law; the most favourable treatment of foreigners for association and wage-earning employment; not less favourable than aliens generally for property, self-employment, housing, higher education): non-discrimination (3), religion (4), personal status (12), property (13-14), access to courts and legal aid (16), employment (17-19), welfare (20-24), identity papers and travel documents (27-28 — the Convention Travel Document), no penalties for illegal entry of refugees coming directly from the territory of persecution who present themselves without delay (31), expulsion only on grounds of national security or public order with due process (32), and **Art 33 — non-refoulement**: 'No Contracting State shall expel or return (refouler) a refugee in any manner whatsoever to the frontiers of territories where his life or freedom would be threatened on account of his race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group or political opinion' — except a refugee whom there are reasonable grounds for regarding as a danger to the security of the country or who, having been convicted by a final judgment of a particularly serious crime, constitutes a danger to the community (33(2)); non-refoulement is now customary international law (UNHCR's 2007 advisory opinion; the Cartagena and OAU instruments; the 2001 Declaration of States Parties) and, through the Torture Convention's Art 3 and the Covenant's Arts 6-7 (Soering, Chahal), applies to everyone facing torture, without the 33(2) exception; **Art 35** — cooperation with UNHCR; **Art 42** — no reservations to Arts 1, 3, 4, 16(1), 33, 36-46. The **Global Compact on Refugees (2018)** on burden-sharing; the **Kampala Convention (2009)** on IDPs; the **Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement (1998, Francis Deng)** — soft law for the 70 million IDPs; the **1954 Convention relating to the Status of Stateless Persons** and the **1961 Convention on the Reduction of Statelessness** (India party to neither; the Assam NRC's 19 lakh excluded persons and the Rohingya are the Indian statelessness questions); the **Palermo Protocols** on trafficking and smuggling (2000)."));
c.push(H3("India and refugees"));
c.push(P("India has hosted refugees continuously — the Partition's 14 million (1947), the Tibetans (1959 — about 1 lakh, with the Dalai Lama's government-in-exile at Dharamsala, registration certificates and the 2014 Tibetan Rehabilitation Policy), the East Pakistani 10 million (1971 — the largest single refugee flow of the century, repatriated after the war), the Sri Lankan Tamils (from 1983 — about 1 lakh in Tamil Nadu's camps and outside), the Chakmas and Hajongs of the Chittagong Hill Tracts (1964 — settled in Arunachal Pradesh), the Afghans (from 1979 — UNHCR-registered in Delhi), the Burmese (Chin, and since 2012 and 2017 the Rohingya — about 40,000, of whom 16,000 are UNHCR-registered), the Bhutanese Lhotshampas (in transit), the Somalis and others — without acceding to the 1951 Convention or enacting a refugee law. The reasons given: the Convention's Eurocentric origin and burden-sharing failure; porous borders and the fear of unlimited obligations; security; the preference for bilateral and case-by-case handling. The **legal regime** is the **Foreigners Act 1946** (Sec 3 — the Central Government's power to make orders on entry, stay and departure; Sec 3(2)(c) — deportation; Sec 14 — punishment for illegal entry), the **Passport (Entry into India) Act 1920**, the **Registration of Foreigners Act 1939**, the **Foreigners Order 1948** (Rule 3 — the power to refuse entry, with the 2017 amendment for the Rohingya), the **Immigration and Foreigners Act 2025** (consolidating these; in force September 2025 — with Sec 3's power to refuse entry, no refugee definition), the **Citizenship Act 1955** (Sec 6A for Assam after the 1985 Accord; the Sec 2(1)(b) proviso and Sec 6B inserted by the **Citizenship (Amendment) Act 2019** — Hindu, Sikh, Buddhist, Jain, Parsi and Christian migrants from Afghanistan, Bangladesh and Pakistan who entered before 31 December 2014 are not 'illegal migrants' and may be naturalised after five years; the Rules of March 2024; the challenge pending in the Supreme Court — the Assam Sec 6A upheld in In re Section 6A, 2024), the Standard Operating Procedure of 2011 (revised 2019) for long-term visas to persecuted minorities and to UNHCR-recognised refugees, and the **courts**: **National Human Rights Commission v. State of Arunachal Pradesh (1996)** — the Chakmas' right to life and to be considered for citizenship protected under Art 21, which applies to non-citizens; the State must protect them against the AAPSU's 'quit' notices; **Ktaer Abbas Habib Al Qutaifi v. Union of India (Gujarat HC, 1998)** — non-refoulement is part of Art 21; two Iraqi refugees to be released to UNHCR; **Dongh Lian Kham v. Union of India (Delhi HC, 2015)** — Burmese Chin refugees not to be deported; **Louis De Raedt v. Union of India (1991)** — a foreigner's Art 21 rights but no right to reside; **Hans Muller of Nurenburg v. Superintendent, Presidency Jail (1955)** — the Government's absolute power to expel foreigners; **Sarbananda Sonowal v. Union of India (2005)** — the IMDT Act struck down; 'external aggression' by illegal migration; **Mohammad Salimullah v. Union of India (2021)** — the Supreme Court declined to stay the deportation of Rohingya detained in Jammu, observing that the right not to be deported is 'ancillary or concomitant to the right to reside or settle' under Art 19(1)(e), available only to citizens, while directing that the deportation follow the prescribed procedure — the ruling that has narrowed the Art 21 non-refoulement reading; the 2024-25 orders on the Rohingya in Delhi and Assam. The Chakma case, Ktaer Abbas and the NHRC's interventions represent the humanitarian strand; Salimullah and the CAA's religious criterion the sovereignty strand; the Asylum Bills of 2015 (Shashi Tharoor) and the Model National Law on Refugees (the Eminent Persons Group, 1997) were never taken up."));
c.push(H3("The other vulnerable groups — in outline"));
c.push(P("**Minorities** — Art 27 ICCPR; the **Declaration on the Rights of Persons Belonging to National or Ethnic, Religious and Linguistic Minorities (1992)**; the Forum on Minority Issues; the Special Rapporteur; the Council of Europe's Framework Convention (1995); in India Arts 25-30, the National Commission for Minorities Act 1992 (the six notified minorities), the Linguistic Minorities Commissioner (Art 350B), T.M.A. Pai (2002) and the minority-institution cases. **Persons with disabilities** — the **Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities (2006; in force 2008; 191 parties; India ratified 2007)** — the social model, reasonable accommodation, accessibility, legal capacity (Art 12), inclusive education, the CRPD Committee; India's **Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016** (21 disabilities, four per cent reservation, the 2017 Mental Healthcare Act's decriminalisation of suicide; Vikash Kumar v. UPSC, 2021; Rajive Raturi v. Union of India, 2024 — accessibility rules mandatory). **Migrant workers** — the **ICRMW (1990; in force 2003; 59 parties, none of the major destination States; India not a party)**; the ILO Conventions 97 and 143; the Global Compact for Migration (2018); in India the Inter-State Migrant Workmen Act 1979 (now the OSH Code) and the Covid-19 migrant crisis (the Supreme Court's 2020-21 suo motu orders). **Older persons** — the Madrid Plan (2002), the Open-ended Working Group; India's Maintenance and Welfare of Parents and Senior Citizens Act 2007. **LGBTQI+ persons** — the Yogyakarta Principles (2006, +10 in 2017), the Independent Expert (2016), Toonen, Navtej Johar (2018), NALSA v. Union of India (2014 — transgender persons' rights, citing the Yogyakarta Principles), the Transgender Persons (Protection of Rights) Act 2019, Supriyo v. Union of India (2023 — no right to same-sex marriage). **Dalits and caste** — the CERD's General Recommendation 29 (2002) on descent-based discrimination, which India contests as inapplicable; the SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act 1989 and the Manual Scavengers Act 2013. **Human rights defenders** — the 1998 Declaration; **journalists** — Security Council Resolution 2222 (2015). **Victims of trafficking and contemporary slavery** — the Palermo Protocol, the Special Rapporteur, India's Immoral Traffic Act, Sec 370 IPC/BNS 143, and the anti-trafficking Bill drafts of 2018 and 2021."));
c.push(CASE("Orissa Mining Corporation v. Ministry of Environment and Forests (2013) 6 SCC 476 (Niyamgiri)","The gram sabhas under the Forest Rights Act and PESA must decide whether the mining infringes the Dongria Kondh's religious and cultural rights; ILO 169, UNDRIP and Saramaka's FPIC standard cited; the Ministry to abide by the decision."));
c.push(CASE("Samatha v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1997) 8 SCC 191","Mining leases in Scheduled Areas to non-tribals (including through the Government) are void under the A.P. Regulation; the Fifth Schedule, Art 46 and the ILO Conventions; tribal land is a source of livelihood and identity."));
c.push(CASE("National Human Rights Commission v. State of Arunachal Pradesh (1996) 1 SCC 742","The Chakma refugees' right to life and liberty under Art 21 protects them against forcible eviction; the State must protect them and process their citizenship applications; Art 21 applies to non-citizens."));
c.push(CASE("Ktaer Abbas Habib Al Qutaifi v. Union of India, 1999 CriLJ 919 (Gujarat HC)","Non-refoulement is encompassed in Art 21 so long as it is not prejudicial to national security; the Iraqi refugees to be handed to UNHCR, not deported."));
c.push(CASE("Mohammad Salimullah v. Union of India (2021) SCC OnLine SC 296","Interim order: no stay on the deportation of Rohingya from Jammu; the right not to be deported is concomitant to Art 19(1)(e), a citizens' right; deportation to follow procedure; the non-refoulement question left open."));
c.push(CASE("Application of the Convention on the Prevention and Punishment of the Crime of Genocide (The Gambia v. Myanmar), ICJ 2020 (provisional measures) and 2022 (jurisdiction)","Myanmar ordered to prevent genocidal acts against the Rohingya and preserve evidence; any State party may bring a claim under the Convention (erga omnes partes); the Rohingya's displacement as the subject of an inter-State case."));
c.push(DIG("The UNHCR's biometric registration (PRIMES) covers the refugees it registers in India; the Forest Rights Act claims are tracked on the Tribal Affairs Ministry's MIS; the Niyamgiri gram sabhas' proceedings were video-recorded under the Court's order; and the Rohingya's identity cards, the NRC's digital exclusion lists and the CAA's online portal are the digital face of the statelessness problem."));
c.push(TIP("Part A: 'ILO Convention 107 vs 169', 'UNDRIP', 'Free, prior and informed consent', 'Samatha case', 'Niyamgiri case', 'Definition of refugee (Art 1A(2))', 'Non-refoulement (Art 33)', 'Exclusion clauses (Art 1F)', 'UNHCR', 'Why India is not a party to the 1951 Convention', 'Chakma case', 'CAA 2019', 'Internally displaced persons', 'CRPD'. Part B: 'Discuss the international law relating to indigenous peoples and examine India's position and law'; or 'Explain the definition of refugee and the principle of non-refoulement under the 1951 Convention, and examine the Indian practice in the absence of a refugee law'; or 'Write notes on the rights of minorities, migrant workers and persons with disabilities'. Part C: a State grants a bauxite lease in a Scheduled Area without consulting the gram sabha — Samatha (void if to a non-tribal), PESA Sec 4, the FRA, Niyamgiri; a Rohingya woman registered with UNHCR is detained for deportation — Salimullah allows deportation by procedure, but Chahal/Art 3 CAT (India signed) and the Gambia v. Myanmar findings support non-refoulement under Art 21 (Ktaer Abbas); a Sri Lankan Tamil refugee in a camp is refused work — no Convention rights, but Art 21 and Art 14 apply to the extent of life and dignity; a person excluded from the Assam NRC has no other nationality — statelessness, the 1961 Convention's standards, the Foreigners Tribunals and the Sec 6A ruling."));
c.push(PB());

module.exports=c;
